==English==

===Etymology===
Medieval English given name from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|Josse}}, name of a seventh century saint Latinized as [[Jodocus]], from {{etyl|br|en}} {{m|br|Iodoc}}, diminutive of {{m|br|iudh||lord}}, from {{der|en|cel-pro|*yowdos}}.<ref> Patrick Hanks and Flavia Hodges: A Dictionary of Surnames. Oxford University Press 1988.</ref>

===Pronunciation===
*: {{rhymes|ɔɪs|lang=en}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{surname|A=An|English and [[Irish]] [[patronymic]]|from=given names}}
# {{given name|female|from=Celtic}}, associated by [[folk etymology]] with [[joy]] and [[rejoice]].

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|Jocelyn}}

====Quotations====
* '''1860''' Mrs Henry Wood (Ellen Wood): ''East Lynne'':
*: "It's a curious name," remarked Captain Levison. "'''Joyce''' - '''Joyce'''! I never heard such a name. Is it a Christian name or a surname?"
*: "She was baptised '''Joyce'''. It is not so very uncommon. Her name is '''Joyce''' Hallijohn. She has been with us several years."
* '''1959''' Anne Sexton: '' The Double Image'':
*: You call me ''mother'', and I remember my mother again,
*: somewhere in greater Boston, dying.
*: I remember we named you '''Joyce'''
*: so we could call you Joy.

